  "headline": "Markets read the Gulf flare-up as an inflation shock, not a growth scare",
  "body": [
    "The configuration is inflation-led tightening. Brent above $104, up 37% in three months and 72% year-to-date, has pushed the 10-year yield to 5.24%, up 41 basis points over the month and near its 52-week high, while the dollar index sits at a fresh 52-week high, up 3.5% on the month. That is the market pricing hotter prices and dearer money, not collapsing demand: copper holds the 91st percentile of its range, up 19% year-to-date, and the copper/gold ratio rose 3% over the month.",
    "The editor's note points to a Saudi-Yemeni escalation and an intraday Brent spike of as much as 5%. The numbers bear out the oil leg, with Brent up 1.7% this week and its realized volatility a quarter above its yearly norm, but not a safe-haven bid. Gold is down 4.9% over the month at the 19th percentile of its range, a bitcoin buys 11% more gold than a month ago, and copper gained 1.2% today. Risk appetite is intact; the conflict is being priced as a supply cost, not a reason to hide.",
    "The signal that carries most weight is the curve: the 30-year minus 10-year spread flattened 9 basis points over the month as the 10-year rose faster than the long end. That is bear-flattening, the market pricing a tighter policy response rather than stronger long-run growth. The dollar's roughly 3% monthly gains against the yen, Canadian dollar and rupee say the same, and that strength explains part of gold's slide in dollar terms."
  ],
